DELETE Records

DELETE removes records from a table.

Delete one student
DELETE FROM students
WHERE id = 5;

Student with id = 5 will be deleted.

⚠️ Important

This is dangerous:

DELETE FROM students;

It deletes all records from the table.

The table itself remains, but its data is removed.

10. ORDER BY

ORDER BY is used to sort records.

Ascending Order
SELECT *
FROM students
ORDER BY marks ASC;

Lowest marks → highest marks.

ASC is the default, so this also works:

SELECT *
FROM students
ORDER BY marks;
Descending Order
SELECT *
FROM students
ORDER BY marks DESC;

Highest marks → lowest marks.

Example
SELECT name, marks
FROM students
ORDER BY marks DESC;

Result:

name	marks
Amit	91
Rahul	85
Rohit	78
Priya	72
Sneha	65

11. LIMIT

LIMIT controls how many records are returned.

Get first 3 students
SELECT *
FROM students
LIMIT 3;
Top 2 students by marks

Combine ORDER BY and LIMIT:

SELECT name, marks
FROM students
ORDER BY marks DESC
LIMIT 2;

Result:

name	marks
Amit	91
Rahul	85

This is a very useful pattern:

ORDER BY marks DESC
LIMIT 2;

Meaning:

Sort by highest marks and return only 2 records.

12. DISTINCT

DISTINCT removes duplicate values from the result.

Find all unique cities
SELECT DISTINCT city
FROM students;

Result:

Pune
Mumbai
Nashik

Although multiple students may belong to the same city, each city appears only once.

Unique courses
SELECT DISTINCT course
FROM students;

Result:

BSc CS
BCA